    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d112">

                <pb facs="0135" n="125" sameAs="frus:frus1942China/pg125"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 Pacific War/2696: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Gauss</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-08-06T20:00:00-04:00">August 6, 1942—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>704. Your 913, 6th. It would be helpful if in your next weekly political report
                    you were to include comment in regard to the reaction on the part of Chinese
                    people in Free China to the recent air activities of the American forces in
                        China.<anchor n="82" xml:id="d112-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d112-f1" n="82" xml:id="d112-f1-content">
                <p>In his telegram No. 918, August 7, 11 a.m., Ambassador Gauss reported to the
                    Department: “With reference Embassy’s 913 of August 6, conversations with
                    officials and information from provinces indicate that spokesman reflects
                    general appreciation of American air assistance. Chinese appear primarily
                    interested in continuation and increase of this type of aid. Official and press
                    satisfaction over American air activities in China has favorable effect on
                    public morale,” (740.0011 Pacific War/2706)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
